    answer: "The answer: Only a professional engineer licensed by the Louisiana Professional Engineering and Land Surveying Board (LAPELS) who is in responsible charge of the work can legally stamp your building plans in Louisiana. The seal must show the engineer's name, Louisiana license number, and the date of sealing, and it certifies that the engineer personally directed the engineering decisions behind the design.\\n\\nLouisiana requires engineering firms to hold a Certificate of Authority from LAPELS and to designate a Louisiana-licensed PE in responsible charge. The board enforces the firm credential actively — out-of-state firms cannot offer or perform engineering in Louisiana on an individual license alone. Apex Grid Engineering assigns Louisiana-licensed engineers as engineers of record on every Louisiana project, so the stamp on your plans always belongs to the engineer who controlled the design, and the firm's authorization is in place before the first submittal.\\n\\nLouisiana's ground conditions make it one of the most demanding stamping environments in the country. Much of the New Orleans metro sits below sea level on compressible, subsiding soils with a water table measured in feet, not tens of feet — shallow foundations are often infeasible, and deep pile foundations, settlement analysis, and dewatering plans are routine parts of the structural package. Hurricanes add the second regime: design wind speeds, wind-borne debris regions, and floodplain administration with freeboard shape every coastal and near-coastal permit set. Baton Rouge, under its consolidated city-parish government, enforces the state-adopted International Building Code with local amendments, and its reviewers see the same high-water-table foundation issues across the parish.\\n\\nLouisiana permits by parish, not just by city. A project in Orleans Parish goes through the City of New Orleans — a consolidated city-parish — with its own safety and permits office, historic district reviews in the older neighborhoods, and strict floodplain administration. The same project in neighboring Jefferson Parish (Metairie, Kenner) goes to Jefferson Parish's permitting offices with different checklists and flood maps. In Baton Rouge, the East Baton Rouge Parish consolidated government covers the city, while projects in Ascension or Livingston Parish go to those parishes' permit offices. The engineer confirms the parish and the municipality before design starts, because the flood maps, the subsidence and soils assumptions, and the review clock all change at the parish line.",
    directAnswer: "Only a Louisiana-licensed PE in responsible charge can stamp your Louisiana building plans, and the firm needs a LAPELS Certificate of Authority. Below-sea-level subsiding soils, high water tables, and hurricanes make Louisiana's foundation and envelope design uniquely demanding.",
